---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';

const emailHref = `mailto:${profile.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Tekmetric × ProofPath — scope a pilot')}`;
const criteria = [
  ['Customer understanding', 'Recognizes the shop owner’s constraints and asks the questions needed to recommend a next step.'],
  ['Product accuracy', 'Checks claims against approved material, identifies unknowns, and avoids unsupported promises.'],
  ['Enablement quality', 'Turns the answer into guidance a sales rep can understand and use.'],
  ['Judgment with AI', 'Shows what was verified, corrected, or rejected before submission.'],
  ['Coaching and adaptation', 'Explains the reasoning, handles an objection, and improves the work after feedback.'],
];
const weeks = [
  ['Week 1', 'Define and calibrate', 'Choose one funded requisition. Agree on the brief, rubric, tools, candidate terms, budget, and success measures. Calibrate reviewers on an example response.'],
  ['Week 2', 'Recruit and qualify', 'Use Tekmetric’s applicant pool and agreed sourcing channels. Confirm interest, role fit, and availability before inviting up to ten candidates.'],
  ['Week 3', 'Run the assessment', 'Provide the same brief and tools, a 48–72 hour written-work window, one feedback round, and a short live coaching exercise. Pay for agreed completion.'],
  ['Week 4', 'Review and decide', 'Deliver evidence packets and a shortlist, capture candidate feedback, and report time and cost. Tekmetric decides whom to advance and whether to repeat.'],
];
---

<Base title="ProofPath for Tekmetric" description="Employer-funded, paid work assessments that reveal candidate judgment and AI aptitude. A Tekmetric pilot with compensation and a capped budget agreed after scoping. By Drew Cleaver." compact noindex>
  <article class="proofpath" aria-labelledby="tekmetric-title">
    <div class="pitch-shell">
      <header class="pitch-hero">
        <p class="pitch-meta">A pilot proposal for Tekmetric <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> By Drew Cleaver</p>
        <h1 id="tekmetric-title">ProofPath</h1>
        <p class="pitch-thesis">The interview should<br />look like the work.</p>
        <p class="pitch-intro">I kept falling short in interviews for roles where I believed I could deliver exceptional results. ProofPath began with a question: why wasn’t that ability coming through?</p>
        <p class="hero-explainer">My working explanation is a mismatch in conditions. Live, on-camera conversations were asking for answers to questions I would normally receive by email—with time to read carefully, think, research, draft, and edit before replying.</p>
        <p class="hero-explainer">At work, I use those steps—and AI—to produce the strongest answer I can stand behind. I choose the words, check the claims, and put my name on the result. A first response on a video call can leave much of that capability unseen.</p>
        <p class="hero-explainer"><strong>ProofPath would make that work visible:</strong> employers fund realistic assignments, candidates are paid to produce useful work, and reviewers assess both the deliverable and the judgment behind it.</p>
        <a class="pitch-link" href="#synthesis">The model in one minute <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        <p class="note">An independent proposal. Tekmetric and HiringCafe have not agreed to participate.</p>
      </header>

      <section class="pitch-section" id="synthesis" aria-labelledby="synthesis-title">
        <div class="section-label"><p>Executive synthesis</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="synthesis-title">See the result. Understand the judgment. Learn what works.</h2>
          <div class="value-list">
            <div><h3>Pay candidates to demonstrate how they work.</h3><p>An employer funds a realistic task with supplied tools, a defined effort cap, and compensation agreed upfront. Candidates have 48–72 hours to return a deliverable. Reviewers can examine the quality of the work and the reasoning behind it, then use live conversation where the role requires it.</p></div>
            <div><h3>Make AI aptitude something people demonstrate.</h3><p>A résumé claim or polished answer reveals only part of the picture. A disclosed assessment workspace can show how candidates frame a problem, prompt, research, verify sources, reject weak output, and revise. Their choices reveal how they turn AI assistance into work they are willing to stand behind.</p></div>
            <div><h3>Use the evidence to improve hiring and learning.</h3><p>With separate research permission, compare work patterns with later onboarding and job outcomes. AI-assisted analysis could surface useful habits, recurring errors, and gaps in understanding. Test those findings in better assessment questions, training materials, and onboarding—not just the next hiring decision.</p></div>
          </div>
          <p>For Tekmetric, the first test is concrete: one sales enablement role, up to ten paid assessments built around shop-owner questions, and a four-week pilot. Its primary purpose is to test whether the evidence improves hiring decisions. Training, onboarding, and research offer additional potential value to investigate.</p>
          <p class="note">The interview mismatch is the hypothesis behind the proposal. The pilot would test the usefulness of the evidence; larger follow-up research would be needed to establish which patterns predict performance.</p>
          <a class="pitch-link" href="#pilot">See the proposed Tekmetric pilot <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" aria-labelledby="fit-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>01</span><p>Why Tekmetric</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="fit-title">Customer understanding should show up in the hiring evidence.</h2>
          <p>Tekmetric connects repair orders, inspections, inventory, and reporting in its <a href="https://www.tekmetric.com/feature/shop-management">shop management platform</a>. Explaining that value calls for product accuracy, commercial judgment, and an understanding of the people running a shop.</p>
          <p>ProofPath would let candidates demonstrate those abilities with the research, AI tools, and revision time available in a working environment. A focused live exercise would still test the interaction the role requires.</p>
          <p class="statement">Can this person help a rep have a better conversation with a shop owner?</p>
          <p class="note">This proposed experiment fits Tekmetric’s published emphasis on customer outcomes, curiosity, and learning from feedback in its <a href="https://www.tekmetric.com/careers">career principles</a>. It does not assume access to, or diagnose, Tekmetric’s current hiring process.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" id="pilot" aria-labelledby="pilot-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>02</span><p>The first trial</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="pilot-title">Start with one sales enablement role.</h2>
          <p>Choose a funded requisition with a hiring manager who wants stronger evidence of candidate ability. Sales enablement is the suggested starting point; Tekmetric would confirm the actual role and its requirements.</p>
          <div class="pilot-math"><p><strong>1</strong><span>funded requisition</span></p><p><strong>10</strong><span>paid assessments maximum</span></p><p><strong>4 weeks</strong><span>target pilot delivery window</span></p></div>
          <p>ProofPath would source and qualify candidates, administer the exercise, pay participants, and deliver a structured shortlist. Tekmetric would retain its applicant system, final interviews, and hiring decision.</p>
          <p class="note">A proposed scope, subject to agreement and candidate availability. The pilot can begin with Tekmetric’s existing pipeline.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" aria-labelledby="task-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>03</span><p>The work sample</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="task-title">Use the questions shop owners actually ask.</h2>
          <p>Tekmetric’s <a href="https://www.tekmetric.com/faq">public FAQ</a> already covers switching systems, training, pricing, and rollout. Those topics provide a starting point for a realistic assessment, refined with the hiring manager’s most common customer questions.</p>
          <aside class="example" aria-labelledby="scenario-title">
            <h3 id="scenario-title">Illustrative scenario: a two-location repair business</h3>
            <p>A shop owner is considering Tekmetric. They want to understand what data can move from their current system, how their staff will learn it, and how to assess value. They are concerned about disruption during the switch.</p>
          </aside>
          <ol class="process-list">
            <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span><div><h3>Prepare a customer response</h3><p>Research the approved product materials, then write a concise follow-up with relevant answers, discovery questions, and a practical next step. Cite sources and flag what needs confirmation.</p></div></li>
            <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span><div><h3>Equip the sales rep</h3><p>Create a one-page coaching guide: what to ask, how to explain the value, and which commitments require specialist input.</p></div></li>
            <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">03</span><div><h3>Respond to feedback</h3><p>Revise one part after a new constraint or reviewer comment. Briefly explain the change and the checks behind it.</p></div></li>
            <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">04</span><div><h3>Coach in conversation</h3><p>Use a short, scheduled role-play to coach a rep through an objection and answer questions about the submitted work.</p></div></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="statement">A realistic exercise produces a deliverable and evidence of how someone works.</p>
          <p>Even with a fictional customer, the response and coaching guide can give reviewers concrete examples of reasoning, communication, verification, and AI use. They can inform follow-up interviews and reveal misunderstandings or skill gaps. With agreed reuse, examples could also inform training, onboarding, and future assessments; their value does not depend on implementing a candidate’s recommendation.</p>
          <p class="note">Use a fictional customer and approved reference materials. A demo environment would be used only if Tekmetric supplies access; no production customer data is needed.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" id="compensation" aria-labelledby="conditions-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>04</span><p>The candidate experience</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="conditions-title">Paid work. Compensation agreed upfront.</h2>
          <dl class="assessment-terms">
            <div><dt>Paid</dt><dd><strong>Matched to the assignment</strong>A stated amount for agreed completion, regardless of score or hiring outcome.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>48–72h</dt><dd><strong>A submission window</strong>The pilot’s total effort cap is two hours, including revision and the live exercise.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Tools + AI</dt><dd><strong>Provided access</strong>The same supported tools, reference packet, and disclosed recording conditions.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>The proposed minimum is $100. Deeper or more specialized assignments warrant higher compensation, with no fixed upper limit. The amount reflects expected effort, complexity, specialization, and any agreed use beyond evaluation. More extensive assignments would require their own scope, effort cap, and compensation.</p>
          <p>Before participation, candidates receive the scope, effort cap, payment amount, and payment timing. Everyone completing the same assignment under the same terms receives the same agreed compensation. The 48–72-hour window allows time to think and schedule the work; it does not increase the two-hour effort cap.</p>
          <p>Evaluation is the initial purpose. Any planned commercial reuse, training use, or research participation would be explained and agreed before candidates begin, with compensation reflecting the arrangement. Additional uses require appropriate permission. Candidates control evidence sharing, subject to Tekmetric’s confidential-material restrictions.</p>
          <p class="note">Tool activity is captured only within the agreed workspace, with recording, access, and retention explained in advance. The pilot would use available tools and managed administration; a universal, fully integrated sandbox is a future product direction.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" aria-labelledby="review-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>05</span><p>The decision evidence</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="review-title">A consistent rubric makes the work easier to compare.</h2>
          <p>Agree on anchored criteria before inviting candidates. Review the output, its supporting evidence, and the response to feedback. Define in advance how material errors and later corrections affect the assessment.</p>
          <dl class="launch-list">
            {criteria.map(([label, description]) => <div><dt>{label}</dt><dd>{description}</dd></div>)}
          </dl>
          <p>Each evidence packet would contain the deliverables, reviewer notes, cited examples, relevant tool-use context, and remaining interview questions. Human reviewers would own the assessment; prompt volume and writing polish would not stand in for job performance.</p>
          <p class="note">Calibrate two reviewers on a sample and independently review a subset to surface disagreements. No universal employability score or automated hiring decision is proposed.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" id="budget" aria-labelledby="budget-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>06</span><p>Budget &amp; ownership</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="budget-title">Scope the work. Agree on a capped budget.</h2>
          <p>Tekmetric and ProofPath would first define the role, candidate count, assignment depth, sourcing needs, review responsibilities, and permitted uses. They would then approve a written scope and capped pilot budget before launch.</p>
          <dl class="launch-list">
            <div><dt>Candidate pay</dt><dd>Compensation identified separately and reserved for participants, based on the agreed assignments and payment terms. Treatment of unused reserves is agreed upfront.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Service delivery</dt><dd>ProofPath’s fee for recruiting, qualification, assessment design, administration, payment coordination, and evidence review.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Tools &amp; direct costs</dt><dd>The supported workspace and other direct delivery costs agreed in the scope.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <div class="value-list">
            <div><h3>Tekmetric sets the hiring standard</h3><p>Provide one accountable hiring manager, a recruiter contact, approved product materials, reviewer availability, and final hiring decisions.</p></div>
            <div><h3>Drew leads the proposed ProofPath pilot</h3><p>Design and administer the assessment, coordinate recruiting and payments, assemble the shortlist, and report delivery time, cost, and lessons.</p></div>
            <div><h3>The employer funds the engagement</h3><p>Candidates pay no participation fee. ProofPath earns its margin through effective delivery of the agreed service, with candidate compensation reserved separately within the budget.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" aria-labelledby="delivery-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>07</span><p>Execution</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="delivery-title">Four weeks from agreed brief to evidence review.</h2>
          <ol class="process-list">
            {weeks.map(([week, title, description]) => <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">{week.slice(-1).padStart(2, '0')}</span><div><h3>{week}: {title}</h3><p>{description}</p></div></li>)}
          </ol>
          <p class="note">This is the delivery target after kickoff. Tekmetric’s hiring timeline and any eventual start date remain separate.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" aria-labelledby="success-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>08</span><p>The pilot scorecard</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="success-title">Agree on what would earn a second pilot.</h2>
          <p>The trial should test whether the evidence is useful and the service is practical. Proposed thresholds give the kickoff a concrete starting point.</p>
          <dl class="launch-list">
            <div><dt>Shortlist quality</dt><dd>Target at least three candidates whom Tekmetric judges worth advancing against the agreed role criteria. Report any shortfall.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Review effort</dt><dd>Target a median of 30 minutes or less for the initial hiring-manager review of each packet. Track setup, live exercises, and later interviews separately.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Candidate experience</dt><dd>Pay every agreed completion on time. Target a median experience rating of at least 4/5 and report the survey response rate.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Cost &amp; repeat demand</dt><dd>Report spend against the agreed budget cap, identify candidate payments separately, and ask whether Tekmetric would fund a comparable engagement.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p>Use a recent comparable requisition as a baseline where available. Capture whether the work samples changed reviewers’ views and gave them better follow-up questions.</p>
          <p class="note">Ten assessments can inform a service decision; they cannot establish predictive validity. With separate permission, later onboarding and role-performance outcomes could inform a larger study.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" aria-labelledby="passport-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>09</span><p>Value beyond one opening</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="passport-title">Let each assessment improve what comes next.</h2>
          <p>ProofPath’s larger model includes a free candidate passport: experience, preferences, optional skill exercises, and work samples with their task briefs, tool conditions, dates, and feedback.</p>
          <p>A candidate could choose to reuse permitted evidence for a relevant future opening. For Tekmetric, the same process could help identify what new hires already do well, where they need support, and which assessment questions produce useful evidence.</p>
          <p>With separate research consent, compare task performance and AI-use patterns with onboarding progress and later role outcomes. AI could help surface recurring verification habits, product misunderstandings, or responses to feedback for human review.</p>
          <p>Turn those findings into testable changes: a clearer assessment question, a coaching exercise that addresses a common error, or onboarding that targets a demonstrated skill gap. Compare the revised approach in later cohorts before treating a pattern as a hiring rule.</p>
          <p class="note">The initial pilot can provide a simple evidence record. A full passport platform would follow validation. Sharing must respect both the candidate’s permissions and Tekmetric’s confidential materials; optional quizzes would not become a universal hiring score.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="pitch-section" aria-labelledby="partnership-title">
        <div class="section-label"><span>10</span><p>The broader partnership</p></div>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2 id="partnership-title">HiringCafe connects discovery. ProofPath adds evidence.</h2>
          <p><a href="https://hiringcafe.com/about">HiringCafe</a> describes a job search engine that collects roles directly from employer websites and rejects pay-to-win discovery. A proposed collaboration could connect that discovery experience to an optional, employer-funded assessment process.</p>
          <dl class="launch-list">
            <div><dt>HiringCafe</dt><dd>Help people discover relevant employer openings, with any additional routing agreed by HiringCafe.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>ProofPath</dt><dd>Qualify interested candidates, administer paid work, and assemble evidence they can choose to share.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Tekmetric</dt><dd>Define the role, fund the assessment, review the evidence, and make the hiring decision.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="statement">A Tekmetric pilot can proceed independently of a HiringCafe partnership.</p>
          <p>After internal validation, Tekmetric could explore a separate offering for interested shop customers—for example, service-advisor hiring built around customer communication and shop workflows. Its <a href="https://www.tekmetric.com/partners">existing partner program</a> provides a context for discussing that possibility.</p>
          <p class="note">No partnership, integration, endorsement, or commercial terms are in place. HiringCafe filters external recruiting-agency listings, so any collaboration would need an agreed direct-employer path. Candidate sharing would be opt-in, and service fees would stay separate from organic search ranking. Expansion to shop customers would require its own validation.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <footer class="pitch-close" aria-labelledby="next-title">
        <p class="pitch-meta">The next step for Tekmetric</p>
        <h2 id="next-title">Choose a role.<br />Scope the first pilot.</h2>
        <p>I’m proposing a working session with a Tekmetric hiring leader and recruiter to select the requisition, pressure-test the exercise, and agree on a funded trial.</p>
        <p>I would lead the design and delivery. Tekmetric would help define what strong work looks like—and decide whether the resulting evidence earns a place in its hiring process.</p>
        <a class="pitch-button" href={emailHref}>Discuss the Tekmetric pilot <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a class="email-link" href={emailHref}>{profile.email}</a>
        <p class="note">Prepared by Drew Cleaver · September 2026. Company context is linked to public sources; the pilot, budget, and partnership paths are proposals.</p>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
